<urn:atm:triage:finding/R3B-FTQ-001>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "R3B-FTQ-001" ;
  triage:title "bin_path runs `cargo build --release -p <pkg> ...` with `Command::output()`. Tha" ;
  triage:description "bin_path runs `cargo build --release -p <pkg> ...` with `Command::output()`. That call has no deadline, no kill and no process-group cleanup. Every other subprocess in workspace-tests goes through spawn_with_deadline, which is bounded. The build uses a fixed shared directory, `$CARGO_TARGET_DIR/workspace-tests-bin` or `target/workspace-tests-bin`. Cargo takes a cross-process file lock on that directory. If another cargo process (a parallel `cargo test` invocation, an IDE or rust-analyzer build, or a stale CI process) holds the lock, the child prints 'Blocking waiting for file lock' and waits indefinitely. A network stall while fetching or a wedged rustc has the same effect. The BUILD_LOCK mutex only serialises threads in one process, so it does not guard against this. The test then blocks forever instead of failing with a message, and CI only stops it at the job-level timeout. Recommendation: Run the build through the bounded runner with a build-specific hard deadline. Take run_with_limits or spawn_with_deadline from tests/support/deadline.rs and give it its own explicit cold-build limit. The 60s SUBPROCESS_DEADLINE is too short for a cold release build, so use a separate named constant rather than quietly widening the shared one. The build must fail with a clear panic that includes the captured stderr and names the file-lock case. Optionally pass --locked, and consider a per-process target directory (for example a tempdir) if sharing the directory is not required." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-a-refine" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "FTQ" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
